Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:02.470 align:start position:0%
 
well the u.s secretary of state anthony  blinken is due to be speaking at the  state department we'll bring you all of  the context and all of that live when it  happens and of course what's it all  about well the final u.s plane is taking  off from kabul the war in afghanistan is  officially over the confirmation came  from the pentagon within the past hour  and a half bringing to an end two  decades of conflict and of course two  weeks of chaos on the ground a c-17  transport plane took off at 1929 gmt and  cleared afghan air space as the  announcement was made  i'm here to announce the completion of  our withdrawal from afghanistan in the  end of the military mission to evacuate  american citizens  third country nationals and vulnerable  afghans the last c-17 lifted off from  hamad karzai international airport on  august 30 this afternoon at 3 29 pm east  coast time and the last manned aircraft  is now clearing the airspace above  afghanistan  we will soon release a photo of the last  c-17 departing afghanistan with major  general chris donahue and the u.s  ambassador to afghanistan ross wilson  aboard  while the military evacuation is  complete the diplomatic mission to  ensure additional u.s citizens and  eligible afghans who want to leave  continues  well more than a hundred thousand people  have left on u.s flights in just the  past two weeks but thousands of afghans  hoping to leave the country now under  taliban control are being left behind  including many who worked as u.s allies  rob mcbride is joining us now live from  kabul  rob it's been a historic moment but also  a solemn moment  absolutely i mean this was uh some  incredible scenes here in kabul um what  is a very historic night the the finally  the ending of 20 years of military  involvement by the united states in the  country words started to spread here  very quickly just after midnight local  time so we were already into the 31st of  august a date which is very important  which will be noted in history books in  years to come this was the date the  deadline set as the departure of the us  and the u.s was good to its word it left  on the 31st but just after midnight into  this day tuesday  and word started to spread on social  media that uh the  the the wheels up were on the last  aircraft taking off from hamid karzai  international airport we started to get  uh some email traffic from the taliban  saying they were taking over control  confirming that the last of the american  aircraft had left then we started uh  interestingly to have something of an  eerie silence we've for days now been  hearing all sorts of aircraft noises of  patrolling warplanes overhead earlier on  monday we saw a b-52 bomber pass over  kabul and then there's been this  thundering background noise of these  aircraft big lumbering c-17s taking off  transport planes taking off from the  airport as this evacuation has continued  and  quite quickly within about 10 minutes or  so as we started to get confirmation  that the last of the americans had gone  this noise started to drift away there  was something of an eerie silence and  then that was quickly replaced by this  rolling gunfire a building cacophony it  began at the airport itself we started  to see tracer gunfire firing up into the  air so it was obviously some sort of  celebratory gunfire and then very  quickly this was picked up across the  city as words started to spread between  taliban groups and taliban fighters in  various locations and very soon the  whole of the skyline around the city was  lit up with guns of all sorts of sizes  and description firing into the air at  this news that finally after 20 years  the u.s have gone  and rob there's been so much attention  leading up to this deadline um this  moment that has now come but i suppose  the inevitable question is what happens  next and of course we're left with a  pretty complex picture  absolutely well for the  taliban  themselves they have to form a  government they've got a a country to  run they unexpectedly  came almost inherited the country with  the collapse of the hamid cars of the  previous ashraf ghani regime very  quickly collapsing two weeks ago they  found themselves in control here in  kabul and very quickly they have had to  run around and are still in the process  of forming uh their government there's  no kind of power sharing that we were  talking about before they are in charge  but they also inherit all of the myriad  problems that comes with afghanistan  after decades of war with all of the uh  problems uh that uh it it has  encountered over the years uh so it has  really got its work to do and the most  important thing that there are a number  of things on that list but one of the  priorities is at the airport itself that  was  seeing the completion of the evacuation  dealing with the chaotic scenes there  and somehow putting an airport back  together again remember this is the  airport at kabul is just about the only  entry and exit point that afghanistan  has really had for what the best part of  two weeks but it's been controlled by  the u.s now the u.s has gone they've  taken air traffic control with it  they're pretty much left a shell of an  airport there it is now up to the  taliban possibly with the help of  foreign partner countries to  re-establish all of the systems that  make an airport work such as its air  traffic control its security and all the  rest it has to  do that the land borders largely remain  closed it has thousands of people uh  moving towards those land borders to try  to get out of afghanistan so it has a  brain drain to deal with it has massive  migrations of people it has to somehow  convince people to stay within the  country and then there are fears from  the people themselves of course that all  of these promises of this being a new  type of taliban regime that it is a  softer more inclusive government it  doesn't mean much  until people see that matched with  actions and a lot of people as we know  simply aren't waiting they are heading  for land borders and they will once they  have the opportunity it seems and we  have airports open and running again be  trying to get onto flights and the other  problem for the united states and for  all of the people who still believe that  they have a right to go to the united  states they they believe they have a  right to protection from the u.s to stop  being uh  rip stop reprisals from the taliban as  they take over because they have some  sort of connection uh with the u.s  military or other foreign forces there  is the problem of how the u.s through  diplomacy through the state department  can try to process those people to try  to leave afghanistan to go to the u.s or  indeed to other countries such as the uk  and that remains a huge problem that  will require all sorts of diplomacy  cooperation from the taliban government  who possibly will not want to contribute  to what is a continuing drain on people  leaving the country that it needs to  make the country work so there's an  awful lot of problems ahead  the airport the departure of the  americans in some ways despite all of  the chaos and the the trauma of the past  couple of weeks that was almost the more  straightforward problem to resolve we  knew it was going to be resolved at  somehow and at some point and it would  be probably resolved on the 31st of  august which it has now in many respects  for many different actors and partners  in the  ongoing afghan crisis the problems  really start  ron mcbride joining me there live from  kabul rob thank you  well the u.s president says he will  address his nation on tuesday but in the  past few minutes the white house has  released a statement saying the past 17  days has seen our troops execute the  largest airlift in u.s history  evacuating over 120 000 u.s citizens  citizens of our allies and the afghan  allies of the united states they've done  it with unmatched courage  professionalism and resolve now our  20-year military presence in afghanistan  has ended  well gabe elizondo was listening to the  pentagon briefing and joins me now live  from washington d.c gabe there was an  extraordinary amount of detail and a  level of frankness wasn't there in that  briefing from the pentagon earlier just  bring us up to date with what happened  in that briefing  yeah that's right uh kenneth mckenzie  the general who's in charge of central  command was very frank  after he announced that all u.s troops  had completely pulled out of the airport  he then followed that up by saying  there's not one single u.s service  member still in afghanistan there was a  lot in that briefing but he confirmed  that since august 14th  the u.s military has helped evacuate  000  civilians including about 6 000 u.s  citizens he did say  not all people that wanted to leave they  were able to get out  we're hearing from the state department  that they believe that there are still  less than 250 u.s citizens in  afghanistan that were not able to get to  the airport uh in the statement by joe  biden that you just read he went on to  also say that  that biden said that it was the  unanimous conclusion from all of his top  commanders that he should not extend  the deadline past august 31st and that  is why the u.s pulled out when it did  mckinsey the general also uh stating uh  that the last military planes that left  hamad kaza international airport carried  the top u.s military general on the  ground as well as  the top u.s diplomat the  ambassador to afghanistan and once that  plane left and it was wheels up  essentially at that point the u.s  military involvement had completely  ended in afghanistan in terms of any  sort of troops or even diplomats for  that matter on the ground he was also  very complimentary quite frankly towards  the taliban saying that the taliban were  in his words and i'll get the exact  words here in his words the taliban were  helpful and useful  over the past several days and weeks in  helping to secure the perimeter of the  airport he said that  the threats against u.s service members  the last few days and weeks did not come  at all from the taliban but instead were  coming from isil k so uh clearly uh uh  mckinsey felt uh  complimentary of the taliban and their  cooperation if you will that cooperation  did not go so far as to where the u.s  told the taliban in advance exactly when  the last planes would be leaving the  airport he said they did not tell the  taliban that  in terms of isil k it was interesting  mckinsey said he believes that there are  about 2 000 isil k fighters  still within afghanistan he insinuated  that a lot of them uh were from us the  prisons the the prisoners that were let  out of some of the jails in afghanistan  he feels some of them  uh were from there and he said that the  taliban would have their hands full  dealing with isil k  in the coming uh  weeks and months quite frankly  though however bottom line to all of  this is the 20-year war in afghanistan  or at least the 20-year presence of u.s  troops in afghanistan  officially is now over gabriel  alessandro they're joining us with the  very latest live from washington d.c  gabe thank you  will u.s secretary of state anthony  blinken is expect

